 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**It's not just you, but it's not because Perth's electricity tariffs have jumped. What's changed is that you're now paying close to the full bill, because the government energy bill credits that used to cut it have mostly stopped.**\n\n**The tariff itself has barely moved.** The ABS CPI shows Perth electricity prices up **2.4% in the year to August 2026**. That matches the small yearly increase in WA's regulated tariff, and it's lower than Perth's overall inflation of **4.3%** over the same year.\n\n**What you actually pay has changed a lot.** The CPI measures what households pay after government bill credits, so it shows when those credits were applied. Here's the Perth electricity index for selected months:\n\n| Period | Perth electricity index |\n|---|---|\n| Aug 2024 – Feb 2025 (several months) | ~26 (credits wiped out most of the bill) |\n| Apr – Jun 2025 | 97.6 |\n| Oct 2025 – Jan 2026 | 69–90 (smaller credits) |\n| Feb – Jun 2026 | 100 (no credits) |\n| Jul – Aug 2026 | **102.4** |\n\nCompared with mid-2024 to early 2025, when the credits covered most of the bill, recent bills are about **4 times higher** on this index. Between February and August 2026 there were no credits at all, so you've been paying the full amount. That's probably why the bills feel so big now. The credits also explain the strange yearly figures for Perth, like +289% in Feb 2026.\n\n**Perth compared with the rest of the country:** Across the capital cities, electricity prices were up **13.2%** in the year to August 2026. That's much more than Perth's 2.4%, because tariffs in the eastern states have risen more. So in tariff terms, Perth has done relatively well.\n\n**What to check on your own bill:** Compare it with the same season last year and see whether there's a credit line on the old bill. If your usage and the credits explain most of the difference, it's the end of the credits you're noticing. If not, look at whether you're using more power (summer cooling, for example) or whether your plan has changed.",
